.TH EKA 1 "October 24, 2026" "eka 0.3.0" "Eka User Manual"
.SH NAME
eka \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eka
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBeka\fR is a replacement for \fBls\fR(1) written in Rust. It lists information about the \fIFILE\fRs (the current directory by default), sorted by name unless another sort option is given, and colors the output when it is written to a terminal.
.PP
Non-directory arguments are listed first, then each directory under a \fIpath\fR:
heading when more than one operand is given or \fB\-R\fR is used. Names are written byte for byte, so names that are not valid UTF-8 are preserved.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Listing"
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Do not ignore entries starting with \fB.\fR, and list the implied \fB.\fR and \fB..\fR.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-almost\-all\fR
Do not ignore entries starting with \fB.\fR, but do not list \fB.\fR and \fB..\fR.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-directory\fR
List directories themselves, not their contents.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR
List subdirectories recursively. Symbolic links to directories are not followed, so link cycles cannot loop.
.SS "Filtering"
.TP
\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\fR=\fIPATTERN\fR
Do not list entries whose name matches the shell \fIPATTERN\fR. May be repeated. It applies even with \fB\-a\fR and \fB\-A\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-hide\fR=\fIPATTERN\fR
Like \fB\-I\fR, but \fB\-a\fR and \fB\-A\fR override it.
.TP
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-backups\fR
Do not list entries whose name ends with \fB~\fR.
.PP
Patterns support \fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB[abc]\fR, \fB[a\-z]\fR, \fB[!abc]\fR and backslash escapes. They are matched against the whole name, a leading dot is not special, and they never apply to files named on the command line.
.SS "Format"
.TP
\fB\-1\fR
List one entry per line. This is the default when standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR
List entries in columns, sorted down. This is the default on a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-x\fR
List entries in rows instead of columns.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR
Fill the width with a comma separated list of entries.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-tabsize\fR=\fICOLS\fR
Assume tab stops at each \fICOLS\fR (default 8) when padding columns; 0 pads with spaces only. Tabs are not used when the width is unlimited (\fB\-w 0\fR).
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-width\fR=\fICOLS\fR
Assume a screen width of \fICOLS\fR columns; 0 means no limit. The default is $COLUMNS, then the width of the terminal, then 80.
.TP
\fB\-l\fR
Use a long listing format: mode, link count, owner, group, size, time and name. There is no header row, and each column is as wide as its widest value. A \fBtotal\fR line gives the allocated size in 1024-byte blocks. A symbolic link is shown as \fIname\fR \fB\->\fR \fItarget\fR.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-numeric\-uid\-gid\fR
Like \fB\-l\fR, but list numeric user and group IDs.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR
Like \fB\-l\fR, but do not list the owner.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR
Like \fB\-l\fR, but do not list the group.
.TP
\fB\-G\fR, \fB\-\-no\-group\fR
With \fB\-l\fR, do not print the group.
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Do not sort, and list all entries. Same as \fB\-aU\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR=\fIWORD\fR
Select the layout: \fBacross\fR or \fBhorizontal\fR (\fB\-x\fR), \fBcommas\fR (\fB\-m\fR), \fBlong\fR or \fBverbose\fR (\fB\-l\fR), \fBsingle\-column\fR (\fB\-1\fR), \fBvertical\fR (\fB\-C\fR).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-human\-readable\fR
With \fB\-l\fR, print sizes as 1.5K, 234M, 2.0G. Sizes are powers of 1024 and are rounded up.
.TP
\fB\-\-si\fR
Like \fB\-h\fR, but sizes are powers of 1000 (1.5k, 234M).
.TP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-classify\fR
Append an indicator to entries: \fB/\fR directory, \fB*\fR executable, \fB@\fR symbolic link, \fB|\fR FIFO, \fB=\fR socket. In the long format a symbolic link shows its target instead of an indicator.
.TP
\fB\-\-file\-type\fR
Like \fB\-F\fR, but do not append \fB*\fR.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-indicator\-style\fR=\fISTYLE\fR
Select the indicator: \fBnone\fR, \fBslash\fR (\fB\-p\fR, append \fB/\fR to directories), \fBfile\-type\fR or \fBclassify\fR (\fB\-F\fR). \fB\-F\fR takes precedence over \fB\-p\fR.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-inode\fR
Print the index number of each file.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-size\fR
Print the allocated size of each file, in blocks, before the name (before the mode with \fB\-l\fR). A \fBtotal\fR line is printed for each directory.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kibibytes\fR
Use 1024-byte blocks. This is the default.
.TP
\fB\-\-block\-size\fR=\fISIZE\fR
Scale sizes by \fISIZE\fR before printing them: the \fBtotal\fR line, the \fB\-s\fR column and the \fB\-l\fR size column. \fISIZE\fR is an integer with an optional unit: K, M, G, T, P, E and KiB, MiB, ... (powers of 1024) or KB, MB, GB, TB, PB, EB (powers of 1000). A unit alone means one of it, and sizes are then printed with its suffix (16K); with a number in front (1K, 2K, 1024) no suffix is printed. \fB\-h\fR overrides this option.
.SS "Sorting"
.TP
\fB\-S\fR
Sort by file size, largest first.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR
Sort by time, newest first.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR
With \fB\-lt\fR: sort by, and show, access time. With \fB\-l\fR: show access time and sort by name. Otherwise: sort by access time, newest first.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR
With \fB\-lt\fR: sort by, and show, status change time (ctime). With \fB\-l\fR: show ctime and sort by name. Otherwise: sort by ctime, newest first.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR
Natural sort of version numbers: runs of digits compare by value, so \fIfile2\fR comes before \fIfile10\fR.
.TP
\fB\-X\fR
Sort alphabetically by extension, then by name.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR
Do not sort; list entries in directory order.
.TP
\fB\-\-group\-directories\-first\fR
List directories, and symbolic links to directories, before other entries. This is done after sorting and reversing, so \fB\-r\fR does not move directories to the end.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
Reverse the order while sorting, including ties.
.TP
\fB\-\-sort\fR=\fIWORD\fR
Sort by \fIWORD\fR instead of name: \fBnone\fR (directory order), \fBsize\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBversion\fR, \fBextension\fR. When several sort options are given the order of precedence is \fB\-\-sort\fR, \fB\-U\fR, \fB\-S\fR, \fB\-t\fR, \fB\-v\fR, \fB\-X\fR.
.PP
Names are compared bytewise, as with \fBLC_ALL=C ls\fR. Ties in size and time are broken by name. Directories are not grouped first.
.SS "Time"
.TP
\fB\-\-time\fR=\fIWORD\fR
Select which timestamp is shown with \fB\-l\fR and used for sorting with \fB\-t\fR: \fBatime\fR (\fBaccess\fR, \fBuse\fR), \fBctime\fR (\fBstatus\fR), \fBmtime\fR (\fBmodification\fR) or \fBbirth\fR (\fBcreation\fR; \fB?\fR when the filesystem does not record it).
.TP
\fB\-\-time\-style\fR=\fISTYLE\fR
Format timestamps in the long listing. \fBfull\-iso\fR is "2026-10-24 09:45:12.123456789 -0600"; \fBlong\-iso\fR is "2026-10-24 09:45"; \fBiso\fR is "10-24 09:45" for recent files and "2026-10-24" otherwise; \fBlocale\fR is the default. \fB+\fR\fIFORMAT\fR uses a \fBstrftime\fR(3) format; if it contains a newline, the part before it is used for older files and the part after it for recent ones.
.TP
\fB\-\-full\-time\fR
Like \fB\-l \-\-time\-style=full\-iso\fR.
.SS "Symbolic links"
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-dereference\fR
For a symbolic link, show information for the file it references. A broken link is still shown as a link.
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-dereference\-command\-line\fR
Follow symbolic links named on the command line.
.TP
\fB\-P\fR
Never follow symbolic links. This is the default and overrides \fB\-H\fR and \fB\-L\fR.
.PP
A command-line symbolic link to a directory is listed as the directory's contents unless \fB\-d\fR or \fB\-l\fR is given.
.SS "Quoting"
.TP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-literal\fR
Print names as they are.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-escape\fR
Print C-style backslash escapes for unprintable characters. Spaces and backslashes are escaped too.
.TP
\fB\-Q\fR, \fB\-\-quote\-name\fR
Enclose names in double quotes, with C-style escapes.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-hide\-control\-chars\fR
Print \fB?\fR instead of unprintable characters. This is the default for the \fBliteral\fR and \fBshell\fR styles when standard output is a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-control\-chars\fR
Print unprintable characters as they are. This is the default when standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-quoting\-style\fR=\fIWORD\fR
Use quoting style \fIWORD\fR: \fBliteral\fR, \fBshell\fR (quote only when the shell needs it), \fBshell\-always\fR, \fBshell\-escape\fR (like \fBshell\fR, with $'...' escapes for unprintable characters), \fBshell\-escape\-always\fR, \fBc\fR or \fBescape\fR. When several options select a style the order of precedence is \fB\-\-quoting\-style\fR, \fB\-Q\fR, \fB\-b\fR, \fB\-N\fR, then \fBQUOTING_STYLE\fR.
.PP
The default style is \fBshell\-escape\fR when standard output is a terminal and \fBliteral\fR otherwise. With the shell styles, names that do not need quotes get a leading space when other names in the listing are quoted, so that names line up. Link targets and the headings printed with \fB\-R\fR or several operands are quoted the same way. A newline in a name is always shown as \fB?\fR when names are printed as they are. Paths in error messages are always quoted.
.SS "Color"
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR[=\fIWHEN\fR]
Colorize names. \fIWHEN\fR is \fBalways\fR, \fBauto\fR or \fBnever\fR. Plain \fB\-\-color\fR means \fBalways\fR. The default is \fBauto\fR: color only when standard output is a terminal.
.SS "Miscellaneous"
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR
Print a help message and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBLS_COLORS\fR
Color specification in the \fBdircolors\fR(1) format. The keys \fBdi ln ex or pi so bd cd fi\fR and \fB*.ext\fR patterns are used on top of the GNU defaults. If it is unset or empty, the built-in scheme is used.
.TP
\fBQUOTING_STYLE\fR
Default for \fB\-\-quoting\-style\fR. Invalid values are ignored.
.TP
\fBTIME_STYLE\fR
Default for \fB\-\-time\-style\fR. Invalid values are ignored.
.TP
\fBCOLUMNS\fR
Line width for the column layouts when \fB\-w\fR is not given.
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
When set and not empty, disables color for \fB\-\-color=auto\fR. \fB\-\-color=always\fR ignores it.
.TP
\fBTERM\fR
The value \fBdumb\fR disables color for \fB\-\-color=auto\fR.
.SH COLORS
Without \fBLS_COLORS\fR, names are colored by type:
.TP
\fBCyan\fR
Directories.
.TP
\fBGreen\fR
Executable files.
.TP
\fBYellow\fR
Symbolic links and FIFOs.
.TP
\fBBold red\fR
Broken symbolic links.
.TP
\fBMagenta\fR
Sockets and images.
.TP
\fBBold yellow\fR
Block and character devices.
.TP
\fBRed\fR
Videos.
.TP
\fBOrange\fR
Audio files.
.TP
\fBBlue\fR
Archives.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success.
.TP
\fB1\fR
Minor problem, such as an entry inside a listed directory that cannot be read.
.TP
\fB2\fR
Serious problem, such as a command-line argument that cannot be accessed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBeka \-l\fR
Long listing of the current directory.
.TP
\fBeka \-lah\fR
Long listing including hidden files, with human-readable sizes.
.TP
\fBeka \-lSr\fR
Long listing, smallest files first.
.TP
\fBeka \-lt\fR
Long listing, newest first.
.TP
\fBeka \-R src\fR
List \fIsrc\fR and all its subdirectories.
.TP
\fBeka \-\-color=always | less \-R\fR
Page colored output.
.SH SECURITY
File names are arbitrary bytes. Printed raw to a terminal, a name can move the cursor, clear the screen, change the window title or, with a bidirectional override, make one file look like another. On a terminal \fBeka\fR therefore quotes names by default (see \fBQuoting\fR). Unprintable characters are control characters, the bidirectional overrides and isolates, the line and paragraph separators, and bytes that are not valid UTF-8. When standard output is not a terminal names are written as they are, as \fBls\fR(1) does, so use \fB\-b\fR, \fB\-Q\fR or \fB\-q\fR when the output is displayed later.
.PP
\fBeka\fR contains no unsafe code. With \fB\-R\fR, a directory that is its own ancestor is reported as "not listing already-listed directory" and skipped.
.SH NOTES
Not implemented yet: \fB\-\-author\fR, \fB\-\-si\fR, and the \fBlocale\fR and \fBclocale\fR quoting styles. The shell styles always use single quotes, where GNU \fBls\fR uses double quotes for a name with an apostrophe. Bidirectional overrides are unprintable in every locale. Extended attribute and ACL markers are not printed. \fBeka\fR runs on macOS and Linux.
